Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for getting a **`.pmtiles`** archive onto a MapLibre GL JS map.  
It works with the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4.0) and the official **PMTiles** JavaScript library (the same code that powers the Protomaps viewer).  

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** | The map engine that will render the tiles. |
| **PMTiles JS library** (`@protomaps/pmtiles`) | Provides a custom URL protocol (`pmtiles://`) that MapLibre can understand. |
| **A `.pmtiles` file** | The vector (or raster) tile archive you want to display. It must be reachable via HTTP(S) (or be bundled with your app). |
| **A web server** (or a local dev server) | Browsers won’t load a file directly from the file‑system due to CORS/security restrictions. |

> **Tip:** If you are using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, Parcel, etc.) you can install the library from npm. If you prefer a quick demo you can also load it from a CDN with a `<script>` tag.

---

## 2. Install / import the PMTiles library

### 2‑a. Using npm / Yarn / pnpm (recommended for a real project)

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
# or
yarn add maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

### 2‑b. Using a CDN (quick demo)

```html
<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

<!-- PMTiles library -->
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js"></script>
```

> The CDN version exposes a global `PMTiles` constructor, so you won’t need an import statement.

---

## 3. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol with MapLibre

MapLibre only knows about `http://`, `https://`, `data:` and a few other built‑in protocols.  
The PMTiles library adds a **custom protocol handler** that tells MapLibre how to fetch tiles from a `.pmtiles` archive.

```js
// If you used the npm version:
import { PMTiles } from "@protomaps/pmtiles";
import maplibregl from "maplibre-gl";

// If you used the CDN version, the global `PMTiles` is already available
// and `maplibregl` is the global MapLibre object.

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Create a PMTiles object that points at your archive
// ---------------------------------------------------
const pmtiles = new PMTiles("https://example.com/path/to/your-data.pmtiles");

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Tell MapLibre how to resolve the custom protocol
// ---------------------------------------------------
maplibregl.addProtocol("pmtiles", (request) => {
  // `request` is an object like { url: "pmtiles://my-archive", ... }
  // The PMTiles instance returns a Promise that resolves to an
  // object { data: ArrayBuffer, cacheControl: string, expires: string }
  return pmtiles.get(request);
});
```

> **What’s happening?**  
> When MapLibre later sees a source URL that starts with `pmtiles://`, it calls the function you registered. The PMTiles instance reads the appropriate byte range from the archive (either from the network or from its internal cache) and hands the raw tile data back to MapLibre.

---

## 4. Create the map

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",          // id of the <div> where the map will live
  style: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json", // any base style you like
  center: [0, 0],            // lon, lat
  zoom: 2
});
```

You can start with any style you like (the default MapLibre demo style works fine).  
The important part is that you’ll **add a new source** that points at the PMTiles archive.

---

## 5. Add the `.pmtiles` source

### 5‑a. Vector tiles (most common)

```js
map.on("load", () => {
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Add a *vector* source that uses the custom protocol
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource("my-pmtiles-source", {
    type: "vector",
    // The part after the double slash is an arbitrary name – it just
    // needs to be unique for this map instance.
    url: "pmtiles://my-pmtiles-source"
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Add a layer that consumes that source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: "my-pmtiles-layer",
    type: "fill",               // or "line", "symbol", "circle", etc.
    source: "my-pmtiles-source",
    // The name of the layer inside the vector tile set.
    // You can discover it with `pmtiles inspect` or by opening the
    // archive in the Protomaps viewer.
    "source-layer": "my_layer_name",
    paint: {
      "fill-color": "#ff6600",
      "fill-opacity": 0.5
    }
  });
});
```

### 5‑b. Raster tiles (if your `.pmtiles` contains raster PNG/JPEG tiles)

```js
map.on("load", () => {
  map.addSource("my-raster-pmtiles", {
    type: "raster",
    url: "pmtiles://my-raster-pmtiles",
    tileSize: 256,          // adjust if your tiles are 512px, etc.
    // optional: minzoom / maxzoom
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: "my-raster-layer",
    type: "raster",
    source: "my-raster-pmtiles",
    paint: {}
  });
});
```

> **How do I know the `source-layer` name?**  
> - Open the `.pmtiles` file in the **Protomaps Viewer** (`https://viewer.protomaps.com/`).  
> - Or run `pmtiles inspect path/to/file.pmtiles` on the command line (the tool is part of the `pmtiles` npm package).  
> - The output lists each layer name and its geometry type.

---

## 6. Full HTML‑only example (CDN version)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<!-- PMTiles library -->
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/protomaps/PMTiles/master/examples/data/nyc.pmtiles");
  maplibregl.addProtocol("pmtiles", (request) => pmtiles.get(request));

  // 2️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: "map",
    style: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json",
    center: [-73.9857, 40.7484], // NYC
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 3️⃣ Add the source & layer once the style is ready
  map.on("load", () => {
    map.addSource("nyc-pmtiles", {
      type: "vector",
      url: "pmtiles://nyc-pmtiles"
    });

    // The NYC demo archive contains a layer called "building"
    map.addLayer({
      id: "nyc-buildings",
      type: "fill",
      source: "nyc-pmtiles",
      "source-layer": "building",
      paint: {
        "fill-color": "#ff6600",
        "fill-opacity": 0.6
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Open the page in a browser and you’ll see the NYC building footprints rendered on top of the base style.*

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & how to troubleshoot

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No tiles appear, console shows `Failed to load source`** | The `pmtiles://` protocol wasn’t registered *before* the source was added. | Move the `addProtocol` call to the top of your script (or before `map.on('load')`). |
| **CORS error when fetching the `.pmtiles` file** | The server hosting the archive does not send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*`. | Serve the file from a server you control (e.g., Netlify, Vercel, GitHub Pages) and add the appropriate CORS header, or proxy it through a CORS‑enabled endpoint. |
| **Tiles look garbled / wrong zoom levels** | The source type (`vector` vs `raster`) does not match the data inside the archive. | Verify the archive’s content with `pmtiles inspect` or the Protomaps viewer. |
| **`source-layer` not found** | Wrong layer name or the vector tiles are in a different *namespace* (e.g., `layer_name` vs `layer_name_0`). | Open the archive in the viewer and copy the exact string shown under “Layers”. |
| **Performance is sluggish** | The archive is being fetched over a high‑latency connection without caching. | PMTiles automatically caches byte‑ranges in IndexedDB. Ensure you’re using a recent browser and that the archive is served with `Cache‑Control` headers that allow caching. |
| **MapLibre throws “Invalid source type”** | You passed a raster archive to a `type: "vector"` source (or vice‑versa). | Switch the source `type` to match the data. |

---

## 8. Advanced options (optional)

| Feature | How to enable |
|---------|---------------|
| **Multiple PMTiles archives** | Create a separate `PMTiles` instance for each URL and register a *different* protocol name (e.g., `pmtiles1://`, `pmtiles2://`). |
| **Custom cache size** | The library uses IndexedDB with a default 500 MiB limit. You can change it via `pmtiles.setCacheSize(bytes)`. |
| **Offline / pre‑loaded archives** | Bundle the `.pmtiles` file with your app and load it via `fetch('mytiles.pmtiles')` → `new PMTiles(arrayBuffer)`. |
| **Using MapLibre’s `source: {url: ...}` with a relative path** | You can also use `url: "pmtiles://my-archive"` *or* `url: "pmtiles://my-archive?key=xyz"` if you need to pass query parameters (the library ignores them). |
| **Styling vector tiles with expressions** | Treat the source like any other vector source; you can use data‑driven styling (`["get", "population"]`, etc.) as long as the attribute exists in the tile. |
| **Debugging byte‑range requests** | Turn on the library’s debug flag: `pmtiles.debug = true;` – it will log each HTTP `Range` request to the console. |

---

